\chapter{Communication and the Shape of Failure}
\label{ch:ipc}
\index{interprocess communication}\index{signals}\index{sockets}\index{TCP}

\section{A pipe is a queue with lifetimes}

The shell pipeline works because the kernel provides a communication
object whose endpoints can be inherited and rebound. One endpoint is
used for reading, the other for writing. The bytes travel through a finite
buffer. The object also records whether readers and writers remain,
which is essential to deciding whether an empty buffer means ``wait'' or
``end of input.''

For a two-stage pipeline, the shell creates the pipe and children, gives
the producer the write end as standard output, and gives the consumer the
read end as standard input. Each participant closes the ends it does not
need. The parent also closes its copies after arranging the children.
Those closes are part of the communication protocol, not optional tidying.

If the parent accidentally retains a write reference, the consumer may
never see end-of-file. If all readers disappear, a writer encounters the
broken-pipe behavior defined by the interface, commonly involving
\texttt{SIGPIPE} and an error. This lets a downstream command stop early
without requiring the producer to know the downstream command's purpose
\parencite[ch. 15]{apue}.

A pipe is a byte stream rather than a sequence of arbitrary application
messages. The interface provides particular atomicity guarantees for
writes up to \texttt{PIPE\_BUF}; it does not promise that every read returns
one original write. Applications needing records must supply framing,
respect the specified limits, and handle short transfers.

\section{Signals announce events, not complete work items}

Signals provide asynchronous notification of selected events.
A user can interrupt foreground work; a timer can expire; a child can
change state. A process may accept a default action, ignore an allowed
signal, or install a handler. Some signals cannot be caught or ignored.

The difficulty is that a handler can run while ordinary code is in a
sensitive state. A library may hold internal locks or be halfway through
updating a data structure. Calling a convenient function from the handler
can reenter code that is not safe to reenter. The set of
async-signal-safe operations is therefore deliberately constrained.

A common strategy is to make the handler do minimal work and arrange for
ordinary control flow to respond. Even setting a flag requires an
appropriate type and protocol. More elaborate designs can route
notifications into an event loop through platform-supported mechanisms.
The aim is to separate notification from work whose context requirements
are stronger.

Ordinary non-real-time signals can coalesce: multiple occurrences do not
necessarily produce an equal number of pending notifications.
An application must not treat them as an unbounded reliable message queue.
This resembles the sleep/wakeup lesson. An event mechanism tells a process
to reexamine state; it does not always preserve every historical occurrence.

\section{Shared memory moves the protocol into the application}
\index{shared memory}

Two processes can communicate by mapping shared storage into their address
spaces. This can avoid repeated copying through a stream interface and
support efficient data structures. It also removes some of the natural
boundaries that make a pipe easy to understand.

The participants must agree on layout, synchronization, ownership,
and recovery if one dies while modifying shared state. A pointer valid
in one address space may not be valid in another unless mappings are
arranged accordingly. Offsets or handles can be safer representations.
A language-level object containing hidden pointers may be unsuitable for
unqualified placement in shared memory.

Memory visibility is another concern. A writer updating fields and then
setting a readiness flag needs ordering that ensures the reader sees
consistent data. Merely observing the same bytes through two mappings
does not supply a complete concurrency protocol. The locks, atomics,
and memory-order rules of \cref{ch:concurrency} return here.

The performance advantage consequently comes with a transfer of
responsibility. The kernel supplies the shared mapping; applications
must supply more of the agreement about meaning. Shared memory is not
a faster drop-in replacement for every pipe or socket.

\section{Sockets generalize endpoints}

A socket has an address family, communication type, and protocol context.
A local Unix-domain socket can connect processes on one host; an Internet
socket can connect through a network. A server commonly binds a name or
address, listens, and accepts connections. Accepting returns a connected
endpoint distinct from the listening endpoint, allowing the server to
continue receiving new clients.

The descriptor representation makes established connections fit familiar
I/O operations. Setup and control remain explicit because networks need
semantics ordinary files do not have. A socket can have a peer, a shutdown
direction, address information, and protocol-specific options.
This is a pragmatic extension of the file interface rather than a proof
that all objects should have identical operations \parencite{unp}.

TCP supplies an ordered reliable byte stream while the connection remains
usable. It does not preserve application message boundaries.
A write of a hundred bytes followed by another write of a hundred bytes
can be read in different-sized pieces. A receiver must accumulate data
according to a framing rule, such as a fixed header containing a length.

Lengths themselves are untrusted data. Before allocating a buffer, the
receiver must check bounds and arithmetic overflow. A peer that declares
a giant message and then sends slowly can consume resources even without
violating the framing syntax. Protocol design therefore includes limits,
timeouts, and cancellation, not merely a diagram of packet fields.

\section{A send is not an acknowledgment of application success}

A successful local send often means bytes were accepted into the local
networking path. It does not prove the remote application parsed them,
committed a transaction, or even remained alive afterward.
Transport acknowledgments and application acknowledgments answer different
questions.

Suppose a client asks a server to charge an account. The server performs
the operation, then the connection fails before the reply reaches the
client. Retrying can duplicate the effect; refusing to retry can leave
the user unable to determine what happened. TCP cannot settle this
ambiguity because the missing fact concerns the application's persistent
state, not just delivery of bytes.

Application protocols can use operation identifiers, deduplication,
idempotent actions, and durable records to make retries meaningful.
Each mechanism has a scope. An identifier remembered only until a process
restarts does not provide the same guarantee as one recorded with the
transaction. ``Exactly once'' requires a precise model of failures and
storage rather than confidence in the network library.

This is where distributed systems depart most sharply from the experience
of a local command pipeline. A local kernel can often know that a process
has exited. Across a network, silence is ambiguous. A timeout is a policy
decision about waiting, not proof that a peer no longer exists.

\section{Event loops and the cost of waiting}
\index{event loops}\index{nonblocking I/O}

A server handling many clients cannot always dedicate an expensive thread
to each idle connection. Readiness interfaces such as select and poll,
and platform-specific mechanisms such as epoll and kqueue, allow it to
wait for activity across many descriptors. Their semantics differ;
readiness and completion are not interchangeable concepts.

Readiness means an operation is likely to make progress under defined
conditions. Another actor can change the state before the operation runs,
so nonblocking code must still handle a result that says to try again
later. A readable descriptor can also indicate end-of-file or an error.
An event loop needs state for partial messages, pending writes, and
connection cleanup.

A design can become difficult to maintain when that state is scattered
among callbacks with unclear ownership. Threads do not automatically solve
the problem; they move the state into stacks and synchronization.
The right choice depends on workload, language facilities, and how clearly
the program can express lifetimes and cancellation.

Unix's communication history is therefore a history of expanding contracts.
Pipes coordinate nearby processes, signals notify, shared memory exposes
common data, and sockets connect endpoints whose failures may be only
partially observable. The interfaces remain compact because they leave
application meaning to the application. Good engineering begins by
recognizing exactly where that responsibility starts.
